A path to becoming a plant/manufacturing/production manager typically involves gaining experience in operations and production roles, such as an engineering technician or production supervisor. Post-graduate education may include a master's degree in business administration or industrial engineering, which can provide a deeper understanding of the business aspects of manufacturing operations. Non-traditional career paths may involve gaining experience in related fields like supply chain management, quality control, or project management. For those looking to become a Chief Operating Officer (COO), additional experience in senior leadership roles such as director or vice president is typically required, along with significant expertise in areas such as operations strategy and process optimization. 
